A||||9||JohnnyK||What sensor is this (on the 4cyl)||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||08-16-2000||05:01 PM||||If you are at the back, facing forward, I unplugged the plug that is directly to the left of the air filter. I unplugged that, and then started the car. The CHeck engine light flashed, and I checked the codes and it said Rich exhaust and Map sensor. I thought that was the idle air control sensor? But could my really rich exhaust be due to a screwed map sensor? In my Haynes manual, it doesn't even show a MAP sensor for the 4cyl... Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Motorhead||08-16-2000||07:49 PM||||That is indeed the IAC.  The Map is attached to the air cleaner housing.  The little black "thingy" on the right side of the air cleaner housing. I actually corrected a code 35 by removing the IAC and cleaning it.  It went away so I assume that fixed it.  Did you get a code 45?  Oxygen sensor.   Seek the Gospel according to Ogre!  I'm sure he can help.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Motorhead||08-16-2000||07:49 PM||||That is indeed the IAC.  The Map is attached to the air cleaner housing.  The little black "thingy" on the right side of the air cleaner housing. I actually corrected a code 35 by removing the IAC and cleaning it.  It went away so I assume that fixed it.  Did you get a code 45?  Oxygen sensor.   Seek the Gospel according to Ogre!  I'm sure he can help.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||08-16-2000||08:02 PM||||I got a code 34 though. But I didn't touch the map sensor. According to the book, my 4cyl doesn't HAVE a MAP sensor, just an MDP sensor? (On page 158 of the haynes manual, the diagram shows no map sensor)...||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||08-16-2000||08:47 PM||||you can ignore a good chunk of what's in haynes. Specifically that diagram on page 158 is wrong. Look in my cave. There is a table of all sensors on all Fiero motors. The link is at the bottom of every page in the forums.<P>All stock Fiero motors have MAP. <P>MAP is a small black device ON the air cleaner housing. It has a plug with 3 wires and a vacuum line connected to the TBI.<P>IAC, on 86 and older 4 cyl TBI uses a square plug with 4 wires.<P>First thing you need to do is make sure everything is pluged in tight. Make sure the MAP vacuum line is not blocked or leaking. THEN reset the ECM.<P>You need to then check the static timing. (That's the "raw" timing of the distributor with no ECM control) If static timing is off then you'll get all kind of problems.<P>If you still get Code 45 is likely Throttle Position Sensor or leaking injector. Could also be O2 but not likely. Likely not IAC either. IAC hardly ever would set only a 44 or 45. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||JohnnyK||08-17-2000||02:03 PM||||How would I go about checking and setting the static timing? (Is that just like checking the normal timing?) THanks||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||08-17-2000||04:07 PM||||That part of the helms book is ok.||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnnyK||08-17-2000||04:32 PM||||Well, I cleared the codes and took it for a drive. No codes now, but is idling roughly at 1500 and still burning rich? ||||reg||1
Z||000008||WKDFIRO||08-18-2000||03:20 AM||||You didn't say what year. In 87-88 the 4 cylinders had Ignition modules that did away with the conventional distributer cap. There is no timing for this type of engine. The modules are constantly changing the timeing when necessary all by them little selves.<BR>      Sounds like its missing? Check the plugs, make sure your wires are okay etc. If you are still having problems eliminate the possibility of internal damage by having the compression checked.<BR>     Good luck.||||reg||5
Z||000009||JohnnyK||08-18-2000||02:12 PM||||Nope, it's an 85. I think I am going to check compression and then do timing. Any other suggestions?||||reg||1
